        <div id="how-it-works" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How a Hot Water Recirculating Pump Works
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every morning, the water sitting in your hot pipes has cooled to room temperature overnight. When you open a faucet, that cold water has to run down the drain before hot water from the heater arrives. In a long ranch house, or a bathroom at the far end of a two-story home, that can mean a minute or more of waiting and several gallons of wasted water. A hot water recirculating pump fixes the wait by keeping, or quickly bringing, hot water close to the faucet.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Basic Loop</h3>

          <p>
            A recirculation system turns your one-way hot water line into a loop. The pump moves water from the water heater, out through the hot pipes, and back to the heater before it has a chance to go cold. Because the loop is kept warm, hot water reaches the tap in seconds instead of minutes. The pump itself is small, typically a fraction of a horsepower, because it is only moving water around a loop, not lifting it or pressurizing the house.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Two Ways to Close the Loop</h3>

          <p>
            The water needs a path back to the heater. Some homes have a dedicated return line, a separate pipe that runs from the end of the hot water system back to the heater. Most homes do not, so retrofit systems borrow the cold water line instead, using a thermostatic valve under the farthest sink that lets cooled hot water cross over into the cold pipe and flow back to the heater's cold inlet. Which of the two your home has decides which pump you buy, so it is the first thing to check.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Controls the Pump</h3>

          <p>
            A pump that runs nonstop wastes heat, because the whole loop loses energy through the pipe walls. So nearly every system has a control: a timer that runs the pump only in the morning and evening, a thermostat that switches it on when the line cools, or an on-demand button or sensor that runs it only when someone asks for hot water. The best setups combine two of these.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Dedicated Return Line or Under-Sink Valve: Which System Fits Your House?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Before you compare pumps, look under the sink farthest from the water heater and at the pipes above the heater. What you find there narrows the list to two or three products.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">If You Have a Dedicated Return Line</h3>

          <p>
            A dedicated return line is usually a third pipe at the water heater, often teed into the drain valve or the cold inlet, sometimes with an old pump or check valve on it. Custom and newer homes with long runs often have one. If you do, buy a bronze or stainless circulator such as the Taco 006-BC7-1IFC or Grundfos UP15-10B7 and add a timer or thermostat. This is the best-performing setup: hot water arrives at every fixture on the loop and the cold line is untouched.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">If You Have No Return Line</h3>

          <p>
            Most existing homes have no return line, and adding one means opening walls. Here, a cold-line system is the practical answer. Either the pump sits at the heater with a sensor valve under the far sink, as with the Grundfos Comfort System, or the pump and valve sit together under the sink, as with the Laing AutoCirc E1 and APCOM UT1. Under-sink pumps need an electrical outlet in the cabinet; heater-mounted pumps need one near the heater.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">If You Have a Tankless Water Heater</h3>

          <p>
            Tankless heaters need a compatible pump or a built-in recirculation mode, because the heater only fires above a minimum flow rate. Use the heater maker's own pump kit, such as the Rinnai GTK15 for compatible Rinnai models, a heater with a built-in pump, or an on-demand kit listed for tankless use such as the PROZRTED HBS24-12. We cover this in detail below.
          </p>

