        <div id="tankless" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Recirculating Pumps for Tankless Water Heaters: Compatibility Comes First
          </h2>

          <p>
            Tankless heaters are the most common reason people search for a recirculating pump, because they add a few seconds of their own delay on top of the cold water in the pipes. They are also where the wrong pump causes the most trouble. If you are still choosing a heater, our guides to the <a href="/best-tankless-water-heater/">best tankless water heaters</a> and <a href="/best-gas-tankless-water-heater/">best gas tankless water heaters</a> note which models have recirculation built in.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Tankless Heaters Are Different</h3>

          <p>
            A tankless heater fires its burner only when water flows through it above a minimum rate, often somewhere around half a gallon per minute, though it varies by model. A small under-sink pump designed for a tank heater may not move enough water to trigger the burner, so it simply circulates lukewarm water. Or it may trigger the burner in short bursts many times an hour, which wastes gas and adds wear. That is why the Laing E1 listing says plainly that it is not for tankless heaters.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Built-In Recirculation</h3>

          <p>
            Several tankless makers now sell models with a pump and recirculation logic built in, including Rinnai and Navien. If you are replacing a heater anyway, this is the simplest route, because the heater manages the pump itself. Some can even work with a cold-line crossover valve, so no return line is needed. Check the installation manual for the exact setup it supports.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Add-On Pump Kits</h3>

          <p>
            If your tankless heater has a recirculation mode but no pump, buy the kit its maker specifies. The Rinnai GTK15 is that kit for Rinnai's Luxury and Ultra series and works with Rinnai's Circ-Logic control on a dedicated return line. Using a third-party pump on a heater whose maker specifies its own can cause control problems and may affect warranty coverage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">On-Demand Pumps</h3>

          <p>
            An on-demand pump runs only when you push a button or a sensor detects someone at the sink, so the heater fires a few times a day instead of all day. That suits tankless heaters well. The PROZRTED HBS24-12 is listed for tank or tankless use; check its flow against your heater's minimum activation flow before you buy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="controls" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Timers, Thermostats and On-Demand Buttons: How to Control the Pump
          </h2>

          <p>
            The control decides how much energy the system uses, so treat it as part of the purchase, not an afterthought.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Timers</h3>

          <p>
            A timer runs the pump during set windows, such as 6 to 9 in the morning and 5 to 10 at night. It is cheap and effective for households with regular routines. The Grundfos TLC and Laing E1 have timers built in; bare circulators like the Taco 006 need a plug-in or hard-wired timer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Thermostats</h3>

          <p>
            A thermostat, or aquastat, switches the pump on when the water in the line cools and off when it is warm again. The Laing E1 uses a fixed 85 to 95 F range. Used with a timer, a thermostat means the pump runs only briefly within each window, which APCOM describes as a few minutes an hour for the UT1.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">On-Demand Controls</h3>

          <p>
            Push-button, remote, motion-sensor and app-controlled systems run the pump only when someone is about to use hot water. They use the least energy and water heating, and they are the best match for tankless heaters, but you have to plan a minute or two ahead. Taco also sells a SmartPlug control that learns when you use hot water, if you are pairing it with a compatible Taco pump.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which Control to Choose</h3>

          <p>
            For a tank heater and a predictable schedule, a timer plus thermostat is the sweet spot. For a tankless heater or an irregular household, choose on-demand. Avoid running any pump 24 hours a day without a control.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="install" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How to Install a Hot Water Recirculating Pump
          </h2>

          <p>
            Under-sink kits are realistic DIY jobs for anyone comfortable replacing a faucet supply line. Dedicated return-line pumps and tankless kits involve soldering, wiring and the heater's controls, so most people hire a plumber. Always follow the maker's instructions and your local plumbing and electrical codes.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Pick the Right Fixture</h3>

          <p>
            Install the under-sink valve or pump at the fixture farthest from the water heater along the hot water run. That makes the whole run between the heater and that sink part of the loop, so every tap along the way benefits.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Shut Off Water and Power</h3>

          <p>
            Close the sink's hot and cold stop valves and open the faucet to relieve pressure. For a heater-mounted pump, turn off the heater's gas or power and close the cold supply valve before you open any connections, and let hot water cool first.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Plumb the Connections</h3>

          <p>
            For under-sink kits, connect the tees to the stop valves, run the braided hoses to the pump or valve, and reconnect the faucet supply lines. For a heater-mounted pump like the Grundfos Comfort System, the pump goes on the hot outlet of the heater, and the sensor valve goes under the far sink. Use the fittings supplied, and do not overtighten compression nuts.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Power and Program</h3>

          <p>
            Plug the pump into a grounded outlet, ideally GFCI-protected if it is near water. Set the timer to your household's routine and the thermostat, if adjustable, to the lowest setting that delivers acceptable hot water.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 5: Test and Check for Leaks</h3>

          <p>
            Open the stop valves, check every joint for drips, then run the system and time how long hot water takes to reach the far tap. Check again for leaks the next day, when the fittings have been through a heat cycle.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="savings" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Do Recirculating Pumps Save Water and Energy?
          </h2>

          <p>
            They save water almost every time. Whether they save energy depends on how they are controlled.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Water Savings</h3>

          <p>
            Every gallon that used to run down the drain while you waited for hot water is saved. Laing and APCOM both cite savings in the thousands of gallons a year for a family of four, and Laing gives a figure of up to 17,000 gallons. Your result depends on how long your runs are and how often you use hot water, so treat those as best-case claims.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Energy Costs</h3>

          <p>
            Recirculation adds two energy costs: the pump's electricity, which is small, and heat lost through the pipe walls as hot water sits in the loop, which can be large if the pump runs all day. A timer, thermostat or on-demand control keeps that heat loss low. Insulating the hot water pipes you can reach, with foam pipe insulation, cuts it further.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When It Pays Off</h3>

          <p>
            A recirculation pump earns its keep in houses with long pipe runs, bathrooms far from the heater, and households that use hot water at predictable times. In a compact home with the heater near the kitchen and bathrooms, the wait may be short enough that a pump adds cost without much benefit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cold-line" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Why the Cold Tap Runs Warm and How to Fix It
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most common complaint about cold-line recirculation systems is warm water from the cold tap at the sink where the valve is installed. It is a side effect of the design, not a defect.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why It Happens</h3>

          <p>
            With no return line, the cooled hot water has to go back to the heater through the cold pipe. So the cold line near that sink fills with lukewarm water. When you open the cold tap, you get a few seconds of that before cold water arrives from the street.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How to Reduce It</h3>

          <p>
            Run the pump on a timer so it is off at the times you want cold drinking water, choose a system with a lower shut-off temperature, such as the Laing E1's 95 F, and make sure the sensor valve is working. A failing valve can let too much hot water cross over. If the problem bothers you, an on-demand system that runs only when asked keeps the cold line cold the rest of the day.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When It Signals a Problem</h3>

          <p>
            If cold taps throughout the house run hot, or hot water appears in toilets, the crossover is letting too much water through, often because a sensor valve has failed open or a check valve is missing. Replace the valve, and check the system's instructions before adjusting anything else.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. What Size Recirculating Pump Do You Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Home recirculation pumps are small because they only move water around a loop. You rarely need to size one from scratch, but a few numbers help.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Pipe Length</h3>

          <p>
            Under-sink kits list the length of piping they are designed for. APCOM rates the UT1 for up to 250 feet of household piping, which covers most single-family homes. If your far fixture is unusually far away, or the house has several branches, a dedicated return line with a larger circulator may serve it better.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Flow and Head</h3>

          <p>
            Flow is how many gallons per minute the pump moves; head is the resistance, in feet, it can push against. Recirculation needs only a modest flow, but tankless heaters need enough flow to fire the burner. Compare the pump's listed figures, such as the PROZRTED's 6 GPM and 39.3 feet, with your heater's minimum activation flow. For bare circulators, check the maker's pump curve.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Multiple Branches</h3>

          <p>
            A single under-sink system covers the fixtures between the heater and the sink it is installed at. Bathrooms on a separate branch of pipe will not benefit. Houses with two or three distinct branches may need a second valve or a balanced return-line system designed by a plumber.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Safety, Codes and Water Quality
          </h2>

          <p>
            A recirculation pump is a low-voltage-risk, low-pressure device, but it is connected to your drinking water and your water heater, so a few rules apply.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Use a Potable-Rated Pump</h3>

          <p>
            Domestic hot water needs a pump made for drinking water: bronze, brass or stainless steel bodies, and lead-free wetted parts. Cast iron circulators are for closed boiler and hydronic heating loops and will rust in fresh water. The PROZRTED kit lists NSF/ANSI 61 and 372 certification; for others, look for lead-free and potable ratings on the maker's spec sheet.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Scald Protection and Water Heater Settings</h3>

          <p>
            Keep the water heater at a safe temperature, commonly 120 F for homes with children or older adults, and do not raise it to compensate for a slow system. If you have a tank, our guides to the <a href="/best-gas-water-heater/">best gas water heaters</a> and <a href="/water-heater-expansion-tank/">water heater expansion tanks</a> cover settings and pressure safety. Check valves in a recirculation loop can affect thermal expansion, so make sure your system still has a working expansion tank where code requires one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Electrical and Codes</h3>

          <p>
            Plug pumps into a grounded outlet, and add one only through a licensed electrician if the cabinet has none. Many areas require permits for return-line and heater-connected work, and some codes require recirculation controls on new installs. Follow the maker's instructions and your local codes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="troubleshooting" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. Troubleshooting a Recirculation Pump
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most problems come down to air, a stuck valve or the wrong settings.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hot Water Still Takes Too Long</h3>

          <p>
            Check that the pump is running in its timer window, that the thermostat has not shut it off early, and that the valve is at the fixture farthest from the heater. On a tankless system, check that the pump moves enough water to fire the burner.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Pump Is Noisy</h3>

          <p>
            Gurgling usually means air in the line; run the taps on the loop to purge it. A grinding or rattling pump may need its cartridge replaced, which bronze circulators like the Taco 006 are designed to allow.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Pump Runs but Nothing Changes</h3>

          <p>
            A clogged sensor valve or a check valve stuck closed stops flow around the loop. Under-sink valves are inexpensive and widely sold as replacements. If you are unsure, call a plumber rather than taking the heater's fittings apart.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Pump Before Checking for a Return Line</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Return-line circulators and under-sink crossover kits are not interchangeable. Look at the pipes at the heater and under the far sink first, then buy the system that matches.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting a Tank-Heater Pump on a Tankless Heater</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many under-sink pumps cannot trigger a tankless burner or will cycle it constantly. Use the heater maker's kit, built-in recirculation or a pump listed for tankless use.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Cast Iron Circulator on Drinking Water</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Cast iron pumps are for closed heating loops and rust in fresh water. Domestic recirculation needs bronze, brass or stainless steel with lead-free wetted parts.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running the Pump 24 Hours a Day</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A pump with no timer, thermostat or on-demand control keeps the whole loop hot all day and wastes heat. Set it to run only when your household uses hot water.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Installing the Valve at the Wrong Sink</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The valve or under-sink pump belongs at the fixture farthest from the heater. Put it closer and the sinks beyond it still wait for hot water.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

