        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            13. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best hot water recirculating pump?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homes with a tank water heater and no return line, the Grundfos Comfort System UP15-10SU7P/TLC is the best hot water recirculating pump: the pump sits at the heater and a sensor valve goes under the farthest sink. Choose the Laing E1 or APCOM UT1 for an all-under-sink install, and a Taco or Grundfos bronze circulator if you have a dedicated return line.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How does a hot water recirculating pump work?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It moves water from the water heater through the hot pipes and back to the heater, so the pipes stay warm and hot water reaches the tap in seconds. The water returns either through a dedicated return line or, in most retrofits, through the cold line via a thermostatic valve under the farthest sink. A timer, thermostat or button controls when it runs.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a return line for a recirculating pump?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Under-sink systems like the Grundfos Comfort System, Laing AutoCirc E1 and APCOM UT1 use the cold water line as the return path, with a valve bridging hot and cold under the farthest sink. A dedicated return line gives the best performance and leaves the cold line cold, but adding one usually means opening walls.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use a recirculating pump with a tankless water heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, but it must be compatible. Tankless heaters fire only above a minimum flow rate, so many tank-heater pumps, including the Laing E1, are not meant for them. Use the heater maker's pump kit, such as the Rinnai GTK15, a heater with built-in recirculation, or an on-demand pump listed for tankless use, like the PROZRTED HBS24-12.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you install a hot water recirculating pump?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For an under-sink kit, shut off the water at the far sink, fit the tees to the stop valves, connect the hoses to the pump or valve, reconnect the faucet lines, plug it in and set the timer. Heater-mounted pumps and return-line systems involve soldering and wiring, so most people hire a plumber. Always follow the maker's instructions and local codes.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a recirculating pump waste energy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can if it runs around the clock, because hot water loses heat through the pipe walls. With a timer, thermostat or on-demand control, the pump runs only briefly, and its own electricity use is small. Insulating accessible hot water pipes reduces heat loss further. The water you stop pouring down the drain is a guaranteed saving.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is my cold water warm after installing a recirculating pump?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Systems without a return line send cooled hot water back through the cold pipe, so the cold tap at that sink can run lukewarm for a few seconds. Use a timer so the pump is off when you want cold water, and check the sensor valve. If cold taps run hot across the house, the valve has probably failed open and needs replacing.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

